import{_ as t,o as i,c as a,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.CcAkc8ux.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/migration.md","filePath":"guide/migration.md","lastUpdated":null}'),d={name:"guide/migration.md"};function o(n,e,l,r,h,c){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<p># Migrating from ImplicitGlobalGrid.jl</p><p>HaloGrids uses explicit <code>Topology</code> and <code>Grid</code> values and per-field halo widths. Read <a href="./concepts">core concepts</a> before converting call sites.</p><p><strong>Check the field layout before changing an exchange call.</strong> Passing a grid is sufficient only when the old send/receive ranges match HaloGrids&#39; ranges. JustRelax&#39;s staggered fields do not all meet that condition.</p><h2 id="What-maps-to-what" tabindex="-1">What maps to what <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-maps-to-what" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What maps to what {#What-maps-to-what}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">ImplicitGlobalGrid.jl</th><th style="text-align:right;">HaloGrids.jl</th><th style="text-align:right;">Conditions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>init_global_grid(...)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>topology(...); grid(t, nlocal)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Supply dimensionality, communicator, and periodicity explicitly.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>update_halo!(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>update_halo!(g, A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Shape equal to <code>g.nlocal</code>; default halo width 1 and overlap 2.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Per-array halo width and overlap</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>field(A; halowidths=(2,2), overlaps=(4,4))</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>overlaps</code> is the array overlap <code>o_A</code> below; it defaults to <code>2*halowidths</code>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nx_g()</code>, <code>x_g(...)</code>, etc.</td><td style="text-align:right;">Explicit <code>(Grid, Field)</code> coordinate helpers</td><td style="text-align:right;">Verify rank stride and physical origin too.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Check-overlap-and-rank-stride" tabindex="-1">Check overlap and rank stride <a class="header-anchor" href="#Check-overlap-and-rank-stride" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check overlap and rank stride {#Check-overlap-and-rank-stride}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For a dimension with base size <code>n</code>, array size <code>m</code>, base overlap <code>o</code>, and exchange width <code>h</code>, ImplicitGlobalGrid derives the array overlap as <code>o_A = o + m - n</code>. Its low send strip starts at <code>1 + o_A - h</code>; its high send strip starts at <code>m - o_A + 1</code>. HaloGrids starts these strips at <code>overlaps - h + 1</code> and <code>m - overlaps + 1</code>, respectively, so pass <code>overlaps = o_A</code> to <code>field</code> and the ranges agree. With the default <code>overlaps = 2h</code> they start at <code>h + 1</code> and <code>m - 2h + 1</code>.</p><p>Both receive into the outer <code>h</code> cells. If <code>o_A &lt; 2h</code>, ImplicitGlobalGrid skips exchange in that dimension; HaloGrids has no equivalent per-field automatic skip based on overlap.</p><p>With JustRelax&#39;s usual <code>init_global_grid(nx, ny, 1)</code> defaults (<code>o=2</code>, <code>h=1</code>), the representative 2D layouts are:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Field</th><th style="text-align:right;">Array size</th><th style="text-align:right;">Effective overlap</th><th style="text-align:right;">Bare-array migration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stokes.viscosity.η</code>, <code>ητ</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(nx, ny)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(2,2)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Exchange ranges match.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stokes.τ.xy</code>, vertex normal stresses, <code>ηv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(nx+1, ny+1)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(3,3)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>overlaps=(3,3)</code> on a grid of its own shape.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stokes.V.Vx</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(nx+1, ny+2)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(3,4)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>overlaps=(3,4)</code> on a grid of its own shape.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>stokes.V.Vy</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(nx+2, ny+1)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(4,3)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>overlaps=(4,3)</code> on a grid of its own shape.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>thermal.T</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(nx+2, ny+2)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(4,4)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>overlaps=(4,4)</code> on a grid of its own shape.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For example, at <code>nx=8</code>, a vertex array has length 9. The old low/high send indices are <code>3</code> and <code>7</code>. HaloGrids with width 1 and the default <code>overlaps=(2,)</code> sends indices <code>2</code> and <code>8</code>; <code>overlaps=(3,)</code> restores <code>3</code> and <code>7</code>. A rank-constant test cannot detect this shift.</p><p>Each distinct shape still needs its own grid to satisfy shape validation. Increasing halo width instead of overlap changes the receive region and number of exchanged cells, so it is not an equivalent migration. The old rank stride is <code>n-o</code>; HaloGrids uses <code>m-overlaps</code>, which is the same when <code>overlaps = o_A</code>.</p><h2 id="A-supported-exchange-pattern" tabindex="-1">A supported exchange pattern <a class="header-anchor" href="#A-supported-exchange-pattern" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A supported exchange pattern {#A-supported-exchange-pattern}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Build a grid once and reuse it. Add <code>overlaps=</code> to <code>field</code> when the layout is not the default <code>2*halowidths</code>:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> HaloGrids</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">import</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> MPI</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># Fresh 2D topology; choose periodicity to match the application.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> topology</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">((</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">); periods</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">false</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">false</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> zeros</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">6</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">g </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> grid</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(t, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">size</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(A))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">f </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> field</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(A; halowidths</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">update_halo!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(g, f)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This demonstrates the exchange API, not a complete JustRelax solver port. Converting <code>_solve_VS!</code> also requires resolving its vertex stress and velocity layouts. A grid based on viscosity cannot accept all their shapes.</p><p>When migrating an existing communicator, explicitly preserve its process dimensions and periodicity. JustRelax&#39;s 2D <code>IGG</code> originates from a 3D MPI topology with a singleton z dimension; do not blindly pass its three-entry <code>dims</code> tuple to a grid holding 2D arrays. Construct a deliberate 2D topology or retain a consistently 3D array representation, and verify coordinate/rank mapping if reordering is enabled.</p><h2 id="Multiple-grids-and-coordinates" tabindex="-1">Multiple grids and coordinates <a class="header-anchor" href="#Multiple-grids-and-coordinates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multiple grids and coordinates {#Multiple-grids-and-coordinates}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each distinct array shape needs its own grid. Grids can share a topology for completed, sequential exchanges. Independent concurrent exchanges need separate topologies with separate communicators; per-grid caches do not isolate MPI tags.</p><p>See <a href="./coordinates">coordinate helpers</a> for explicit coordinate APIs. Verify the old rank stride and location-dependent origin before substituting those helpers. Matching function names does not establish matching coordinates.</p><p>Fields with <code>overlaps != 2*halowidths</code> are supported by <code>update_halo!</code>, the face exchange and the coordinate helpers, but not by <code>save_hdf5</code>/<code>load_hdf5!</code> or <code>save_pvtk</code>, which reject them. <code>nlocal_from_global</code> assumes the default overlap. Verify any migrated call site with a position-encoded test, not a rank-constant one.</p>`,21)])])}const k=t(d,[["render",o]]);export{g as __pageData,k as default};
